Subject: mysys: my_readlink treats a directory as not a symlink on OpenVMS

OpenVMS readlink() fails with ENOENT on an existing directory, where POSIX
and regular files give EINVAL (probe on x86 with the D6 features: file
EINVAL, directory ENOENT with or without ./ or a trailing slash, symlinks
to files and directories read correctly). my_readlink() takes only EINVAL
as "not a symlink", so rm_dir_w_symlink() failed and DROP DATABASE stopped
with "Can't read value for symlink './db' (Errcode: 2)" after deleting the
tables, leaving the database directory. On ENOENT, an lstat() that finds a
non-symlink now counts as EINVAL.

--- a/mysys/my_symlink.c	2026-10-09 12:43:38.600188926 +0100
+++ b/mysys/my_symlink.c	2026-10-09 12:43:46.175549073 +0100
@@ -23,6 +23,9 @@
 #include <sys/param.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #endif
+#ifdef __VMS
+#include <sys/stat.h>
+#endif
 
 static int always_valid(const char *filename __attribute__((unused)))
 {
@@ -55,7 +58,20 @@
   if ((length=readlink(filename, to, FN_REFLEN-1)) < 0)
   {
     /* Don't give an error if this wasn't a symlink */
-    if ((my_errno=errno) == EINVAL)
+    my_errno= errno;
+#ifdef __VMS
+    /*
+      OpenVMS's readlink() fails with ENOENT, not EINVAL, on an existing
+      directory (regular files give EINVAL): DROP DATABASE stopped with
+      "Can't read value for symlink './db'" after deleting the tables.
+    */
+    {
+      struct stat st;
+      if (my_errno == ENOENT && !lstat(filename, &st) && !S_ISLNK(st.st_mode))
+        my_errno= EINVAL;
+    }
+#endif
+    if (my_errno == EINVAL)
     {
       result= 1;
       strnmov(to, filename, FN_REFLEN);
